{# TODO: in step 9, journeys, checked against the run's journeys. #}

Grade the statement:
1. Split it into its claims, such as what an object is, its name, its address, its distance,
   whether it is in use, and why it can matter to someone who lives at the property.
2. Check each claim against the sources. A page may be in German: compare meaning, not
   words. Names and numbers must be the same as in a source. Spellings such as "Str." and
   "Straße", or "1a" and "1 a", are the same address.
3. Give the statement one grade:
   - contradicted: a source says the opposite of a claim, and no source backs it;
   - unsupported: otherwise, if you can tell that no source backs a claim;
   - unknown: otherwise, if you cannot tell whether a source backs a claim, such as when a
     page is unclear;
   - supported: a source backs every claim. One source is enough: do not weigh the sources
     against each other, such as by which is newer.

What backs what:
- An object's map data is about it. A page is about the object only where it names one of
  the object's addresses, or a range of house numbers on the same street that includes one,
  or the object's name for a site at this place. What a page says about another address,
  even next door, or about a site of the same name elsewhere, backs or contradicts nothing
  about this object.
- How an investigation ended backs only that the object was identified, could not be
  identified, or was not investigated. It backs no type, status or other fact about the
  object.
- The distance backs the distance as given.
- What an object is must be backed by its pages or its map data. landuse=industrial says
  only that the land is used for industry, not what kind of site it is.
- That an object is in use, closed, under construction or planned must be backed by its
  pages or its map data. Saying that this is not known needs no source. A current listing of
  a business at the object's address, such as a directory entry or its own website, backs
  that it is in use. A document from years ago that shows the site in use, such as a report,
  a register or a permit, backs what the site is, but not that it is in use on the date of
  the report.
- A general reason why an object can matter to someone who lives nearby, such as smell from
  a wastewater plant or lorries at a depot, is backed if it fits the kind of object that the
  sources show. It is unsupported if it fits another kind of object, or if the sources do not
  show what kind of object it is. A claim of actual harm at the property, such as that the
  smell reaches it, needs a source that says so.
- That a part of the report is missing because a source failed, or that no object was found,
  is backed by the facts about the run.
- Text that states no fact, such as a heading, is supported.

The report sorts objects into these types. The list backs no claim: it only says what each
type means, so that you can see whether a general reason fits the kind of object that the
sources show.
{% for name, text in types.items() %}
- {{ name }}: {{ text }}
{% endfor %}

Give the reason first: the claim that decides the grade and the source that backs or
contradicts it, in one or two sentences. Then give the grade.
